WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.960
And it shows I got a ton done, like an unbelievable amount done.

00:04.960 --> 00:09.360
This is the closed PRs in Lakebed where, reminder, I'm still the only contributor.

00:09.360 --> 00:11.960
I have not forced this project onto my team yet.

00:11.960 --> 00:13.160
They don't deserve the slop.

00:13.160 --> 00:14.600
It's cleaning up fast.

00:14.600 --> 00:15.600
Thank you, Fable.

00:15.600 --> 00:20.360
But all of these PRs were done in literally two to three days.

00:20.360 --> 00:24.600
The sheer volume of work here, especially if you're looking at the ones that actually

00:24.600 --> 00:29.960
merged, like this pile here, it's like, I think, 11 or 12 PRs that merged all

00:29.960 --> 00:34.880
Well, not just in one day, but from one thread.

00:34.880 --> 00:39.080
And I keep going and I'm on page two, we're still in things from two days ago, then there's

00:39.080 --> 00:44.800
a four day ago, one five days ago, then a huge jump to two weeks, because I was just

00:44.800 --> 00:46.080
not really feeling it.

00:46.080 --> 00:50.040
When I lost access to this model, I tried to run around in circles and get the best stuff

00:50.040 --> 00:53.600
I could out of Opus, but it wasn't at the bar I needed to ship.

00:53.600 --> 00:58.360
It what ended up happening is in this particular project, Lakebed, I had a bunch of these PR

00:58.360 --> 01:03.760
up of work that was like 50 to 80 percent complete, but I didn't like the SDK changes

01:03.760 --> 01:04.760
it made.

01:04.760 --> 01:09.160
I didn't really have confidence in the implementation, including it up would just take too long.

01:09.160 --> 01:13.000
And the result was that I kept having ideas, I kept making PRs, and they kept sitting there

01:13.000 --> 01:14.000
doing nothing.

01:14.000 --> 01:20.320
I probably had 20 or 30 PRs just sitting in this repo, not moving, and the project has

01:20.320 --> 01:22.160
been sitting there as a result.

01:22.160 --> 01:23.160
And I don't like that.

01:23.160 --> 01:24.960
I really want to ship Lakebed.

01:24.960 --> 01:26.760
I've talked about what Lakebed is in some other videos.

01:26.760 --> 01:29.680
I don't want to bog you down the details until I actually have it launched.

01:29.680 --> 01:31.200
I haven't earned the right to market this yet.

01:31.200 --> 01:31.920
I want it done.

01:32.040 --> 01:33.520
So we'll do that later.

01:33.680 --> 01:37.600
But I want to talk about the workflows that got me this far in the project,

01:37.640 --> 01:41.000
as well as some of the cool things I'm doing on other projects that I've been working on.

01:41.320 --> 01:44.560
I'm going to start in a weird place here, but I promise it will help.

01:44.560 --> 01:48.640
I'm going to start with min-maxing tips and like cost reduction things in order

01:48.640 --> 01:52.320
to make sure that when you adopt this workflow, you don't immediately bankrupt

01:52.320 --> 01:55.800
yourself, because if you were to go to the second half of this video and skip

01:55.800 --> 01:59.960
straight to that part, it will be expensive. That's what I did in the first three days.

